@* ===== Food & Culture: alternating image / text sections ===== *@
<section class="section" id="food-culture">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="section-head">
            <span class="eyebrow">Food &amp; culture</span>
            <h2>What to eat, drink and know</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="split">
            <figure>
                <img src="~/images/food.svg" alt="Khorovats barbecue skewers served on freshly baked lavash" />
                <figcaption>Lavash, khorovats and fresh herbs — the basis of any Armenian table.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <div class="split__text">
                <h3>Food</h3>
                <p><strong>Khorovats</strong> — Armenian barbecue of pork, lamb or chicken grilled over vine cuttings — is the national dish and the centre of every celebration.</p>
                <p><strong>Dolma (tolma)</strong> are grape or cabbage leaves stuffed with spiced minced meat and rice, served with garlic yoghurt.</p>
                <p><strong>Lavash</strong>, the paper-thin flatbread baked on the walls of a clay <em>tonir</em> oven, was inscribed by UNESCO in 2014 on the list of Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. Look also for <em>gata</em> sweet bread, dried fruit, walnuts in grape syrup (<em>sujukh</em>) and the summer apricots that gave the country its colour.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="split split--reverse">
            <figure>
                <img src="~/images/wine.svg" alt="Glasses of red Areni wine in front of a vineyard" />
                <figcaption>Wine has been made in the Areni valley for around 6,000 years.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <div class="split__text">
                <h3>Drink</h3>
                <p>Armenia is one of the oldest wine regions on Earth. The Areni-1 cave in Vayots Dzor contains the world's oldest known winery, dated to roughly 4100 BC, and the Areni Noir grape is still grown on the same slopes today.</p>
                <p><strong>Armenian brandy</strong> — most famously ArArAt, produced by the Yerevan Brandy Company — is world-renowned; the distillery in Yerevan offers tours and tastings.</p>
                <p>Also try <em>tan</em> (a salty yoghurt drink), fruit vodkas, and strong Armenian coffee brewed in a small pot.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="split">
            <figure>
                <img src="~/images/culture.svg" alt="Carved letters of the Armenian alphabet at the Alphabet Monument" />
                <figcaption>The Armenian alphabet, created in 405 AD, is still in daily use.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <div class="split__text">
                <h3>Culture &amp; etiquette</h3>
                <p>Armenia became one of the world's first Christian nations in the early 4th century, and faith, family and the written word shape daily life. The unique alphabet and the <em>khachkar</em> cross-stones are national symbols.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Dress modestly in churches and monasteries: cover shoulders and knees; men remove hats, and in a few active monasteries women may be asked to cover their head.</li>
                    <li>Hospitality is serious: accept the coffee, expect a toast, and never leave a host's table hungry.</li>
                    <li>Toasting is led by a <em>tamada</em> (toastmaster) — wait for the toast before drinking.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
